// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/vfs/mount.vuma
// VWK VFS Subsystem — Mount + MountTable + lifecycle (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the K5e layer of the VWK kernel VFS: the Mount (a mounted
// filesystem at a dentry — the kernel's per-mount metadata, mirroring
// Linux's `struct mount`) and the MountTable (a 16-slot array of Mounts
// stored as parallel flat byte arrays — VUMA has no struct arrays). K5c
// created tmpfs; K5d created initramfs. This file (K5e) provides the
// mount layer that tracks which filesystems are mounted where, so K6
// (syscall layer) can wire sys_mount / sys_umount2 to vfs_mount /
// vfs_umount, and so the path-walking layer (K5b's namei + a future
// follow_mount) can resolve "across the mount point" by consulting
// vfs_mounted_at.
//
// ── What a Mount is ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A Mount binds a mounted filesystem's superblock (sb) to a dentry in the
// parent filesystem's dentry tree — the dentry IS the mount point. When a
// path walk (K5b's namei) reaches a mount-point dentry, it consults
// vfs_mounted_at to find the mounted filesystem's root dentry and
// continues the walk there. Linux calls this "crossing a mount point";
// the dentry that the FS is mounted on is `mnt->mnt_mountpoint`, and the
// root dentry of the mounted FS is `mnt->mnt_root`. This simplified VWK
// Mount only stores the mount-point dentry index + the mounted FS's sb
// address — the mounted FS's root dentry is recoverable from its
// superblock (K5b's superblock layout stores root_dentry as a field),
// so we don't duplicate it here.
//
// ── Mount layout (4 fields, 32 bytes) ───────────────────────────────────────
//   dentry (u64) — dentry INDEX (NOT a pointer — PMT discipline) where
//                  the FS is mounted. This is the mount-point dentry in
//                  the parent filesystem's DentryTable. A value of 0
//                  doubles as the "free slot" marker (see "Why dentry == 0
//                  as free marker?" below). Stored as an index into the
//                  DentryTable (0..63).
//   sb     (u64) — superblock ADDRESS (for the mounted FS). K5b's
//                  superblock allocator returns a State<Superblock>; we
//                  store its address here so a future path walker can
//                  follow the mount by reading sb->root_dentry. (PMT
//                  discipline permits storing State addresses in u64
//                  fields — the value is opaque, never dereferenced by
//                  the mount layer itself. K6's sys_mount will set this
//                  to the sb returned by K5c's tmpfs_mount / K5d's
//                  initramfs_mount.)
//   parent (u64) — parent mount INDEX (or 0 for the root mount — the
//                  root mount is its own parent, mirroring Linux's
//                  `mnt->mnt_parent == &root_mnt` convention). Stored as
//                  an index into the MountTable (0..15). The root mount
//                  is the one mounted at the root dentry (dentry 0 in
//                  a fresh DentryTable); all other mounts are descendants
//                  of the root mount. A future follow_mount can walk
//                  parent links to enumerate the mount tree.
//   next   (u64) — next sibling mount INDEX (or 0 for the last sibling).
//                  Stored as an index into the MountTable (0..15). The
//                  sibling chain links all mounts sharing the same parent
//                  — e.g. all filesystems mounted at the top level of
//                  the root filesystem are siblings via their `next`
//                  links. (Linux uses a `mnt_hash`-indexed list per
//                  mountpoint; the simplified `next` chain is enough
//                  for K5e's purposes — K6's sys_mount can prepend to
//                  the chain by setting `next = parent's first child`.)
//
// ── Why sb == 0 as the free-slot marker? ────────────────────────────────────
// The K5e contract literally specified `mount_get_dentry(tbl, i) == 0`
// as the free-slot test in mount_find_free. That marker is provably
// broken: dentry index 0 is the ROOT dentry, so the root mount (which
// is the very first vfs_mount call, made by kmain at boot) has
// dentry = 0. After the root mount is installed at slot 0, slot 0's
// dentry field IS 0 — so the contract's `dentry == 0` test would
// consider slot 0 "free" again and reuse it for the next mount,
// clobbering the root mount. The K5e self-test exercises exactly this
// case: it mounts at dentry 0 first (the root), then mounts at dentry 5,
// expecting slot 1 — which is impossible under the contract's literal
// `dentry == 0` marker.
//
// We resolve this by using `sb == 0` as the free-slot marker instead.
// A valid mount ALWAYS has a non-zero sb (the superblock address is a
// non-null State<Superblock> returned by K5b's sb_alloc / K5c's
// tmpfs_mount / K5d's initramfs_mount — never 0). So sb == 0 unambigu-
// ously means "this slot is not holding a mounted filesystem." This
// choice:
//   • Keeps the contract's MountTable layout byte-identical (no extra
//     `used` flag — unlike K5a's FileTable, which added a `used` byte
//     array to resolve the same kind of ambiguity for File slots; K5e
//     avoids the extra field by reusing sb, which is never 0 for a
//     valid mount).
//   • Keeps the contract's self-test passing as written (mount at
//     dentry 0 first → slot 0; mount at dentry 5 next → slot 1).
//   • Requires only four localized code deviations from the contract:
//       - mount_table_init zeros sbs (instead of / in addition to
//         dentries) so every slot starts with sb == 0 (free).
//       - mount_find_free tests `mount_get_sb(tbl, i) == 0` (instead
//         of `mount_get_dentry(tbl, i) == 0`).
//       - vfs_umount zeros sb (in addition to dentry) so the slot
//         becomes free, and filters its scan by `sb != 0` so it does
//         not match an already-free slot whose dentry happens to be 0.
//       - vfs_mounted_at filters its scan by `sb != 0` so it does not
//         match an already-free slot whose dentry is stale or 0.
//   • Is semantically cleaner than adding a `used` flag: sb is ALREADY
//     the "is this a real mount" indicator (no valid mount has sb = 0),
//     so reusing it as the free marker costs nothing.
//
// ── MountTable layout (4 + 1 fields, 517 bytes) ─────────────────────────────
//   dentries ([u8; 128]) — 16 slots * 8 bytes = 128 bytes. The dentry
//                           field of each Mount slot, packed little-
//                           endian. Accessed via mount_get_dentry /
//                           mount_set_dentry (8-iteration pack/unpack).
//   sbs      ([u8; 128]) — 16 slots * 8 bytes = 128 bytes. The sb field.
//   parents  ([u8; 128]) — 16 slots * 8 bytes = 128 bytes. The parent
//                           field.
//   nexts    ([u8; 128]) — 16 slots * 8 bytes = 128 bytes. The next
//                           field.
//   count    (u32)        — number of Mount slots currently in use
//                           (0..16). Bumped by vfs_mount, decremented
//                           by vfs_umount. Used by K6's sys_mount to
//                           return -ENFILE (0 - 23) when full.
//
//   Total size: 128*4 + 4 = 516 bytes (padded to 520 by the codegen's
//   max-field-alignment rule — 4 bytes of trailing padding after the u32
//   to align the next slot to 8 bytes).
//
// ── Why parallel flat byte arrays instead of [Mount; 16]? ───────────────────
// VUMA's parser does NOT support struct arrays (`[Mount; 16]` doesn't
// parse — only `[u8; N]` parses). So the MountTable stores each Mount
// field as a separate [u8; 128] array, and the per-slot offset is
// `idx * 8`. This is the same pattern as K5a's InodeTable /
// DentryTable / FileTable (parallel flat byte arrays with per-slot
// offset = `idx * width`).
//
// ── Helper inventory ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   mount_get_dentry(tbl, idx) -> u64   — read Mount[idx].dentry (u64)
//   mount_set_dentry(tbl, idx, val)     — write Mount[idx].dentry (u64)
//   mount_get_sb(tbl, idx) -> u64       — read Mount[idx].sb (u64)
//   mount_set_sb(tbl, idx, val)         — write Mount[idx].sb (u64)
//   mount_get_parent(tbl, idx) -> u64   — read Mount[idx].parent (u64)
//   mount_set_parent(tbl, idx, val)     — write Mount[idx].parent (u64)
//   mount_get_next(tbl, idx) -> u64     — read Mount[idx].next (u64)
//   mount_set_next(tbl, idx, val)       — write Mount[idx].next (u64)
//   mount_table_init(tbl)               — zero all 16 slots, count = 0
//   mount_find_free(tbl) -> u32         — first slot with dentry == 0
//                                          (16 if full)
//   vfs_mount(tbl, dentry, sb) -> u32   — allocate slot, set dentry + sb
//                                          (parent = next = 0), bump count;
//                                          return slot (16 if full)
//   vfs_umount(tbl, dentry) -> i64      — find slot with matching dentry,
//                                          zero it, decrement count;
//                                          return 0 on success,
//                                          0 - 1 (= -1 as i64) if not
//                                          mounted
//   vfs_mounted_at(tbl, dentry) -> u32  — find slot with matching dentry;
//                                          return slot (16 if not mounted)
//
// ── Decimal constants ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per the K5e contract's "Use decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants use DECIMAL (8, 16, 1000, 2000, etc.) — decimal avoids the
// parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties
// — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale,
// and K5a's file.vuma for the same rule applied to slot widths and
// self-test values). The -1 sentinel is written as `0 - 1` (NOT as the
// literal `18446744073709551615`) — VUMA misinterprets that literal,
// and `0 - 1` goes through flatten_expr's BinOp::Sub path which produces
// the correct -1 as i64 (bit pattern 0xFFFF...FFFF). This matches the
// convention in K4d's wait.vuma (`return 0 - 11; // -EAGAIN`).
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Layout declarations.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Mount: a single mounted filesystem, stored as 4 u64 fields. This
// layout is for documentation — the actual MountTable uses parallel
// flat byte arrays (VUMA has no struct arrays — see "Why parallel flat
// byte arrays?" in the file header). K6 (syscall layer) and a future
// follow_mount will re-declare this layout byte-identically.
layout Mount = {
    dentry: u64,
    sb: u64,
    parent: u64,
    next: u64,
}

// MountTable: 16 Mount slots stored as parallel flat byte arrays (VUMA
// has no struct-array support — see "Why parallel flat byte arrays?"
// in the file header). Each u64 field is a separate [u8; 128] array
// sized for 16 slots * 8 bytes. `count` tracks how many slots are in
// use (0..16). The free-slot marker is dentry == 0 (see "Why dentry ==
// 0 as free marker?" in the file header for the rationale and the
// known root-mount-at-dentry-0 limitation).
layout MountTable = {
    dentries: [u8; 128],
    sbs: [u8; 128],
    parents: [u8; 128],
    nexts: [u8; 128],
    count: u32,
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// u64 pack/unpack helpers (dentries, sbs, parents, nexts).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 8-byte little-endian pack/unpack. Byte-identical pattern to K2a's
// pool_get_base / pool_set_base (pmm.vuma), K3e's syscall_table_get /
// syscall_table_set (table.vuma), K4a's pt_get_vruntime / etc.
// (task.vuma), K5a's inode_get_ino / etc. (inode.vuma), K5a's
// dentry_get_parent / etc. (dentry.vuma), and K5a's file_get_dentry /
// etc. (file.vuma): 8-iteration while loop, shift by i*8, mask 255 on
// writes, sum-of-shifted-bytes on reads.

// Read the u64 `dentry` field of Mount slot `idx` from MountTable.dentries.
fn mount_get_dentry(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.dentries[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `dentry` field of Mount slot `idx` into MountTable.dentries.
fn mount_set_dentry(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.dentries[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `sb` field of Mount slot `idx` from MountTable.sbs.
fn mount_get_sb(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.sbs[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `sb` field of Mount slot `idx` into MountTable.sbs.
fn mount_set_sb(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.sbs[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `parent` field of Mount slot `idx` from MountTable.parents.
fn mount_get_parent(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.parents[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `parent` field of Mount slot `idx` into MountTable.parents.
fn mount_set_parent(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.parents[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `next` field of Mount slot `idx` from MountTable.nexts.
fn mount_get_next(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.nexts[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `next` field of Mount slot `idx` into MountTable.nexts.
fn mount_set_next(tbl: State<MountTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.nexts[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Lifecycle primitives.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// mount_table_init: zero all 16 sbs (which marks every slot FREE —
// see "Why sb == 0 as the free-slot marker?" in the file header) and
// all 16 dentries (so vfs_mounted_at's `sb != 0` guard sees a fully
// clean table even before the first mount), and set count = 0.
// Idempotent. Called by kmain at boot before the first vfs_mount (K6's
// sys_mount is the first caller; the root mount is the very first
// vfs_mount call, establishing the root filesystem).
//
// Does NOT zero parents / nexts — those are populated by vfs_mount's
// caller (K6's sys_mount sets parent + next), so zeroing them here
// would be wasted work (and VUMA has no memset). mount_find_free only
// inspects sbs, and vfs_mounted_at / vfs_umount filter by sb != 0, so
// zeroing sbs (and dentries, for vfs_mounted_at's pre-first-mount
// cleanliness) is enough to make every slot look free.
//
// NOTE: this deviates from the K5e contract, which specified zeroing
// only dentries. That would be insufficient under the sb == 0 free
// marker (the sbs array would hold garbage, and any non-zero garbage
// byte at offset 0 of a slot's sb field would make that slot look
// IN USE to mount_find_free). Zeroing sbs is required for the free
// marker to work; zeroing dentries too is a defensive belt-and-
// suspenders measure that costs only 16 extra 8-byte writes at boot.
fn mount_table_init(tbl: State<MountTable>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 16 {
        mount_set_dentry(tbl, i, 0);
        mount_set_sb(tbl, i, 0);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    tbl.count = 0;
}

// mount_find_free: find the first FREE slot (sb == 0), return its
// index. Returns 16 if the table is full (the caller MUST check — K6's
// sys_mount returns -ENFILE = 0 - 23 in that case). The lowest-free-
// slot-wins policy matches Linux's find_first_zero_bit and K5a's
// file_alloc / dentry_alloc / inode_alloc.
//
// NOTE: this deviates from the K5e contract, which specified testing
// `mount_get_dentry(tbl, i) == 0`. That marker is broken for root
// mounts (dentry 0 is the root dentry, so the root mount's slot has
// dentry = 0 and would look free). See "Why sb == 0 as the free-slot
// marker?" in the file header for the full rationale. The sb == 0
// test is unambiguous because a valid mount always has a non-zero sb.
fn mount_find_free(tbl: State<MountTable>) -> u32 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 16 {
        if mount_get_sb(tbl, i) == 0 {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 16;
}

// vfs_mount: mount a filesystem at `dentry_idx` with superblock address
// `sb`. Allocates a free Mount slot (mount_find_free), sets dentry + sb,
// leaves parent + next at 0 (the caller — K6's sys_mount — sets parent
// to the enclosing mount's index and prepends to the sibling chain via
// mount_set_next; for the simple K5e self-test, parent = next = 0 is
// fine). Bumps count. Returns the slot index, or 16 if the table is
// full.
//
// Does NOT check whether `dentry_idx` is already a mount point — K6's
// sys_mount is responsible for that check (calling vfs_mounted_at first
// and returning -EBUSY = 0 - 16 if already mounted). The K5e layer
// keeps the primitive simple: mount_find_free + set fields + bump count.
fn vfs_mount(tbl: State<MountTable>, dentry_idx: u64, sb: u64) -> u32 {
    let slot = mount_find_free(tbl);
    if slot == 16 {
        return 16;
    }
    mount_set_dentry(tbl, slot, dentry_idx);
    mount_set_sb(tbl, slot, sb);
    mount_set_parent(tbl, slot, 0);
    mount_set_next(tbl, slot, 0);
    tbl.count = tbl.count + 1;
    return slot;
}

// vfs_umount: unmount the filesystem mounted at `dentry_idx`. Finds the
// in-use slot whose dentry matches (linear scan — 16 slots is small
// enough that a hash table would be overkill), zeros the slot's dentry
// AND sb (which marks it FREE for the next mount_find_free under the
// sb == 0 free marker), and decrements count. Returns 0 on success, or
// 0 - 1 (= -1 as i64) if no filesystem is mounted at `dentry_idx`. K6's
// sys_umount2 calls this; on -1 it returns -EINVAL = 0 - 22 to
// userspace.
//
// The `0 - 1` form (NOT the literal `18446744073709551615`) avoids the
// parser's signed-literal path — see "Decimal constants" in the file
// header. The function's return type is i64, so `0 - 1` is interpreted
// as -1 in i64 (bit pattern 0xFFFF...FFFF), which the caller compares
// against 0 to detect "not mounted" (the K5e self-test does exactly
// this: `if ret2 == 0 { return 9; }`).
//
// The scan filters by `sb != 0` (slot is in use) BEFORE testing the
// dentry match. This is required because a FREE slot's dentry field
// might be 0 (if it was never used) or stale (if it was umounted
// without zeroing dentry); without the sb != 0 guard, a vfs_umount(tbl,
// 0) call on a table with free slots would erroneously match a free
// slot (whose dentry happens to be 0) and underflow count.
//
// NOTE: this deviates from the K5e contract in two ways: (1) it filters
// the scan by `sb != 0` (the contract had no filter), and (2) it zeros
// sb in addition to dentry (the contract zeroed only dentry). Both
// deviations are required by the sb == 0 free marker — see "Why sb == 0
// as the free-slot marker?" in the file header. Zeroing dentry too is
// defensive: it makes vfs_mounted_at's pre-mount state cleaner (a free
// slot's dentry is 0, not stale).
//
// Does NOT zero parent / next — they stay stale until vfs_mount reuses
// the slot (vfs_mount writes all four fields, so the stale values are
// overwritten before use). This matches K5a's file_free (which only
// clears `used`, leaving the other File fields stale).
fn vfs_umount(tbl: State<MountTable>, dentry_idx: u64) -> i64 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 16 {
        if mount_get_sb(tbl, i) != 0 {
            if mount_get_dentry(tbl, i) == dentry_idx {
                mount_set_dentry(tbl, i, 0);
                mount_set_sb(tbl, i, 0);
                tbl.count = tbl.count - 1;
                return 0;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// vfs_mounted_at: check whether a filesystem is mounted at `dentry_idx`.
// Linear scan of all 16 IN-USE slots (filtered by `sb != 0`); returns
// the slot index of the matching mount, or 16 if not mounted. K5b's
// namei / a future follow_mount calls this when crossing a dentry to
// decide whether to follow the mount (if 16, no mount — continue the
// walk in the current FS; if 0..15, follow the mount by reading sb's
// root_dentry and continuing the walk there). K6's sys_mount also
// calls this as the "already mounted?" guard before calling vfs_mount.
//
// The scan filters by `sb != 0` (slot is in use) BEFORE testing the
// dentry match. This is required because a FREE slot's dentry field
// might be 0 (the sentinel value that mount_table_init writes) —
// without the sb != 0 guard, vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0) called on a table
// with free slots would erroneously match a free slot (whose dentry
// is 0) and return its index instead of 16. The K5e self-test's
// check 5 exercises exactly this case: after umounting the root mount
// (which zeroes slot 0's sb AND dentry), vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0) must
// return 16 (not 0).
//
// NOTE: this deviates from the K5e contract, which had no `sb != 0`
// filter. The filter is required by the sb == 0 free marker — see
// "Why sb == 0 as the free-slot marker?" in the file header.
//
// Note: returning 16 (not 0xFFFFFFFF) for "not mounted" matches the
// K5e contract and is consistent with mount_find_free's "table full"
// sentinel — both use 16 (the slot count) as the out-of-band value,
// since valid slot indices are 0..15.
fn vfs_mounted_at(tbl: State<MountTable>, dentry_idx: u64) -> u32 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 16 {
        if mount_get_sb(tbl, i) != 0 {
            if mount_get_dentry(tbl, i) == dentry_idx {
                return i;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 16;
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Self-test (the K5e DoD).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Allocate a MountTable via state_new, init it via mount_table_init, then:
//   (1) vfs_mount(tbl, 0, 1000) → returns 0 (first slot); tbl.count == 1.
//   (2) vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0) → returns 0 (slot 0 holds the mount at
//       dentry 0 — confirms the dentry field was written and the linear
//       scan finds it).
//   (3) vfs_mount(tbl, 5, 2000) → returns 1 (second slot); tbl.count == 2
//       (confirms mount_find_free skips slot 0 after step 1 and finds
//       slot 1).
//   (4) vfs_umount(tbl, 0) → returns 0 (slot 0 unmounted); tbl.count == 1
//       (confirms the linear scan finds the matching dentry and the
//       count decrement works).
//   (5) vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0) → returns 16 (no longer mounted at dentry
//       0 — confirms the umount zeroed the dentry field).
//   (6) vfs_umount(tbl, 99) → returns 0 - 1 (= -1 as i64, not 0 — confirms
//       the not-found path returns the sentinel and not a bogus 0).
// Returns 0 on success, 1..9 on the corresponding check failure (so a
// future CI failure pinpoints the broken check).
//
// The 1000 / 2000 sb values are non-trivial u64 values (greater than
// 255, with non-zero high bytes) — they would catch any pack/unpack
// bug in mount_set_sb / mount_get_sb, though the K5e self-test does
// not read sb back (K6's follow_mount will). The dentry values 0 and 5
// exercise both the low-byte-only path (0, 5) and the "already used"
// path (slot 0 reused after umount in a real K6 scenario).
fn main() -> i32 {
    let tbl = state_new(MountTable);
    mount_table_init(tbl);

    // Check 1: mount tmpfs at dentry 0 (root) → slot 0; count == 1.
    let m0 = vfs_mount(tbl, 0, 1000);
    if m0 != 0 { return 1; }
    if tbl.count != 1 { return 2; }

    // Check 2: vfs_mounted_at(0) → slot 0 (it's mounted there).
    let found = vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0);
    if found != 0 { return 3; }

    // Check 3: mount another FS at dentry 5 → slot 1; count == 2.
    let m1 = vfs_mount(tbl, 5, 2000);
    if m1 != 1 { return 4; }
    if tbl.count != 2 { return 5; }

    // Check 4: umount dentry 0 → 0; count == 1.
    let ret = vfs_umount(tbl, 0);
    if ret != 0 { return 6; }
    if tbl.count != 1 { return 7; }

    // Check 5: vfs_mounted_at(0) → 16 (no longer mounted at dentry 0).
    let found2 = vfs_mounted_at(tbl, 0);
    if found2 != 16 { return 8; }

    // Check 6: umount non-existent dentry 99 → 0 - 1 (not 0).
    let ret2 = vfs_umount(tbl, 99);
    if ret2 == 0 { return 9; }

    return 0;
}
